<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kinkaku-ji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Golden Pavilion, Rokuon-ji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kinkaku-ji, the Temple of the Golden Pavilion, is a Zen temple in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It began as the retirement villa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>, who built the gold-leafed pavilion from 1397; after his death in 1408 it became a temple. The original pavilion was burned down by a monk in 1950 and rebuilt in 1955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji.jpg" alt="Kinkaku-ji" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nacaru · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Pavilion_Kinkaku-ji_water_mirror_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site was first a villa called Kitayama-dai, belonging to the statesman Saionji Kintsune. In 1397 the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a> bought it from the Saionji family and turned it into the Kinkaku-ji complex. When he died, his son converted the building into a Zen temple, according to his wishes. The gold was meant to mitigate and purify pollution and negative feelings about death. During the Ōnin war of 1467 to 1477 every building of the complex except the pavilion burned down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pavilion has three storeys, and each is built in a different style. The ground floor, the Chamber of Dharma Waters, is in shinden-zukuri, the residential style of the Heian aristocracy, with unpainted wood and white plaster. The second, the Tower of Sound Waves, is in the style of warrior aristocrats and holds a Buddha Hall and a shrine of the goddess of mercy, Kannon. The third, the Cupola of the Ultimate, is in Chinese Chan style. The upper two floors are covered in gold leaf, and the pavilion serves as a shariden, housing relics of the Buddha. A bronze phoenix tops the roof. It was an important model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ginkaku-ji">Ginkaku-ji</a> and Shōkoku-ji in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p>The pavilion stands in a strolling garden and extends over a pond called Kyōko-chi, or Mirror Pond, which holds ten smaller islands. Rocks, bridges and plants are arranged to recall notable places in Chinese and Japanese literature. The grounds were laid out after descriptions of the Western Paradise of the Buddha Amida. The garden is regarded as a good example of Muromachi period design.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>On 2 July 1950 a 22-year-old novice monk, Hayashi Shoken, burned the pavilion down. The original statue of Yoshimitsu was lost. The monk was sentenced to seven years, released in 1955 because of mental illness and died of tuberculosis in 1956. Yukio Mishima's 1956 book The Temple of the Golden Pavilion fictionalises the events. The present pavilion, 12.5 metres high, dates from 1955 and is said to be a close copy, though some question whether the original had so much gold leaf. In 1984 the gold leaf was found peeling and it was replaced in 1986 and 1987. The temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, one of the Historic Monuments of Ancient Kyoto.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kinkaku-ji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
